1
00:00:01,100 --> 00:00:07,100
Worked example: edit the coordination code and run free checks in the actual notebook. No agents run in this segment.

2
00:00:07,100 --> 00:00:13,100
The input is a real archived MBTA schedule. A 24:08 departure belongs to the previous service day.

3
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:18,200
Start by checking the behavior the app must preserve. The starter function is deliberately incomplete.

4
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:23,000
The free check reports the missing implementation. It does not make a model request.

5
00:00:23,800 --> 00:00:30,800
Require the actual trip, service date and civil departure time together. This is the worked answer.

6
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:37,000
The real row passes. A missing departure and the wrong service day fail. These are useful negative controls.

7
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:46,400
Complete both halves of the worker loop: build its request, then dispatch only an allowed action and return the result.

8
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:53,200
The free check exercises those functions with stub tools. Starting the real coding worker is a separate action.

9
00:00:53,800 --> 00:00:58,800
Now write the coordinator. A task can start only after its dependencies are integrated.

10
00:00:59,600 --> 00:01:06,600
The readiness check and atomic claim serve different purposes: dependencies first, then one owner.

11
00:01:07,400 --> 00:01:13,400
Run the real dependency and competing-claim checks. The integration task must stay blocked until its inputs are ready.

12
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:21,900
Retrieve accepted lessons for this scope and interface version. Proposed notes and old rules stay out of the worker request.

13
00:01:22,700 --> 00:01:28,700
A useful-looking note is not automatically evidence. The later comparison tests whether the checked lesson changes the work.

14
00:01:30,900 --> 00:01:36,900
Compare the actual departure IDs in order. The final evaluation also checks the full output with an independent scorer.

15
00:01:37,800 --> 00:01:44,800
This is the reference app running locally, not an agent result. The rider chooses a platform and a historical time.

16
00:01:44,900 --> 00:01:50,900
At Park Street after midnight, the board includes the 00:08 train from the previous service day.

17
00:01:50,900 --> 00:01:56,900
The follow-up feature finds a direct journey and preserves an arrival after midnight. It does not plan transfers.

18
00:01:56,900 --> 00:02:02,900
The itinerary is an actual output file. Worker builds must reproduce the required behavior before release.
